# INTENTIONALLY EMPTY - no BlobStore is registered by this module.
#
# AzureBlobStore used to be listed here. It was removed in sprint-23
# (Story 1.2) for three reasons, any one of which is sufficient:
#
#  1. NOT CONSTRUCTIBLE. Its only constructor takes a BlobServiceClient
#     (AzureBlobStore.java:38), so ServiceLoader raised
#     ServiceConfigurationError on every discovery pass. AutoConfig
#     swallowed that error before Story 1.1, so nothing ever said so.
#
#  2. IT IS A SKELETON. Seven of its eight BlobStore methods throw
#     UnsupportedOperationException (AzureBlobStore.java:51-84); only
#     exists() is implemented. A DISCOVERABLE skeleton is worse than no
#     registration at all: auto-config would hand a caller an object that
#     satisfies the contract's type and fails on first real use.
#
#  3. REGISTERING IT WOULD BREAK CLASSPATHS THAT WORK TODAY. Since
#     Story 1.1, AutoConfig.blobStore() fails fast when more than one
#     BlobStore provider is available and no CULVERT_BLOBSTORE_PROVIDER
#     selector chose between them (AutoConfig.java:357-362). GcsBlobStore
#     and S3BlobStore both register; S3BlobStore declines outside AWS
#     through ProviderAvailability.isAvailable() (S3BlobStore.java:108)
#     rather than by throwing. A no-arg AzureBlobStore WITHOUT that opt-out
#     would turn today's silent no-op into a hard IllegalStateException for
#     every user carrying both the GCP and Azure jars. So "just add a no-arg
#     constructor" is not the smaller change here - it is the breaking one.
#
# Re-register only when the adapter is complete AND implements
# ProviderAvailability (com.enrichmeai.culvert.autoconfig) so it declines
# outside Azure, the way S3BlobStore does. The expansion is tracked at
# https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert/issues/18.
#
# The class is unchanged. Construct it explicitly:
#     new AzureBlobStore(serviceClient)
